Common A/B Testing questions - Design, Sample Size & Tesy Duration, Causal Inference, Multiple Testing, Launch Decision

Designing an A/B Test involves selecting the right metrics, the right randomization units, calculating the sample size and test duration, as well as determining the minimum detectible effect.

An A/B test is an experiemnt where all elements are constant except one variable. Typically, we comapre a control group against test group. All variables are identical between the two groups except for one which is being tested. 

Different versions of a product are called variants
When there are two variants it is called A/B Test. More than 2 variants - A/B/N Test

The goal of running an A/B test is to make data-driven decisions only when the results are reliable and reproducible
A/B tests(randomized controlled experiment) are the best way to establish causality with high probability
To make the results reproducible an important requirement is that the factor we rae testing is the cause of the change in the metric. change to product -> change to metric
So when we launch the feature to the wider audience the impact can be predicted based on the treatment effect seen in the experiment 


5 major steps in running an A/B Test - 

STEP 1: Prerequisites  
        - Before running an experiment few things have to be ready:
          We need to define the key metrics to measure the goal of the metric - Overall Evaluation             Criteria(it should be agreed upon by different stakeholders and should be practically                measured)
        - Changes should be easy to make
        - Have enough randomization units - to be assigned to different variants. Most commonly used           randomization unit is user. Recommendation is have 100s because the larger the sample size the smaller the effects can be detected

STEP 2: Experiment Design
        - What population of randomization units should be select? Do we want to target a specific             population or all users? Sometimes its useful to run experiments only on a certain segment           because the change affects only to that segment(eg - geography)
        - Size of an experiment: We need to compute the sample size for the experiment in order to             achieve the required statistical power
        - How long to run the experiment? We'll need to consider seasonality, day of week effect,              primacy and novelty effect(all of this will influence how long the experiment is run)
Step 3: Running Experiment
Step 4: Result to Decision
        - After running the experiment for the required duration, we'll check and interpret the                results and use them to make the decision. 
          First step is to do sanity checks to make sure the data is reliable. We continue with                analysis once the sanity checks are passed. If it fails, we discard the result and look into root cause and we may need to re run the experiment. 
          If the test is passed, we make decision based on - 
          Tradeoffs between different metrics, cost of launching a change(new change may introduce             complexity and bugs to the code base and and maintenance can be costly, opportunity                  cost(the time and effort we spend launching a change might not be as much as the                     opportunity cost of giving up a different idea). 

          When costs are high we have to ensure that the benefits outweigh the costs. We set a                 practical significance boundary to reflect those costs and we only launch a product if the           result is significant. 
          When costs are low, we launch any positive change as long as the results are statistically           significant.

Step 5: Post-Launch Monitoring
          We need to monitor the long-term effect after launch because the short-term effect can be            different from long-term effect due to different reasons



What metrics to select?

Think about what the user journey looks like. The funnel mostly wants to nudge/push users to take some actions which when taken will be a success for us. This can help come up with success metrics for the experiment.
Three kinds of operational metrics that companies use to measure success, progress, and areas of improvement-

Goal metric/Success/True North/OKR/Primary metric - reflects a company's long term vision and always ties to a company's mission. They are a small set of metrics that a company truly cares about. Simple to communicate to stakeholders. It should also be stable over a long period of time to allow the organization to work towards improving it. They may be critcal to measure the overall success of the company, they may not be suitable to measure the success of an experiment as they might be difficult to measure or may not be sensitive enough to product changes 

Driver metric/surrogate/indirect/predictive metric - are often used to measure short term objectives. They align with the goal metrics but are more sensitive and actionable to be able to make major short-term progress and drive teams to work on it. Therefore, better than goal metrics for A/B testing.  

Guardrail metric - guard us from harming the business and violating important experiment assumptions. They can be split into two -
organizational guardrail metrics: If this metric shifts in negative direction, the business suffers significant loss. Eg- page load latency(commonly used), errors per page, client crashes
Trustworthy related metrics - they are used to monitor the trustworthiness of an experiment that is to check if there is any violation of assumptions eg - randomization units assigned to variants are truly random(sample mismatch ratio - numbers assigned to the variants are different. Perform t-test or chi-test to check if the assignment ratio matches with what was designed. 

A good metric should be simple, easy to understand, clear so that there is no abiguity in interpretation, actionable

Formulating metrics for online experiment - 

3 attributes of metrics that can be used for A/B tests:
Measurable - should be able to calculate the metric with the data collected during the experiment period
Attributable - we should be able to attribute the metric values to the experiment variant, meaning we should be able to calculate metrics for different variants independently(meaning can you assign the effect(change in metric) that you are seeing with the treatment that you have applied). 
Sensitive and Timely - metrics should be sensitive enough to detect changes in a timely manner; metric should be measurable in a short time

In online experiments we select a few driver metrics and a few guardrail metrics to monitor impact on other aspects of the business

Combine a few driver metrics into an Overall Evaluation Criterion(OEC) - weighted combination of driver metrics 
No more than 5 driver metrics
Having too many metrics may confuse people and may lead to ignoring the key metrics. It may also affect the decision-making process and increases the chances of having a false discovery

For interviews, understand the goal of the experiment and then think of metrics that focus on measuring that. Another thing is to focus on metrics tracking the difference in user experience between both variants. 
How to make a launch decision when one metric goes up and one metric goes down?
Have to consider tradeoffs between different metrics
eg - user acquisition & revenue
Such decisions are to be discussed with all stakeholders to come to a decision


Randomization Unit - 

who/what we assign randomly to each variant; what we select as randomization unit impact user experience and metrics used
UserID is the most common - consistent user experiment and allows for long-term measurements
Others can be cookie(can identify users without logging in but dont persist across browsers and platforms, temporary as they can expire or cleared by users), device ID(immutable ID associated with mobile phones so commonly used for changes in mobile app), event such as page view/session(doesn't require user to log in, each page view is a separate randomization unit, doesn't distinguish between users; similarly sessions generally expire after 30-mins of inactivity, one user can be assigned to different variants). Since number of events is always higher than number of users, using events gives us more power to detect small changes but it can also lead to inconsistent user experience so if the change is visible to users it is better to not use event as the randomization unit.

Considerations when choosing randomization units - 
Consistent user experience(for any design changes we use user ID or cookie as randomization unit). In experiments where users aren't logged in, we often use cookies as the randomization unit. On the first visit, we assign the user to a variant and store it in a cookie so the experience stays consistent across sessions. It’s great for A/B testing design or layout changes and works especially well for anonymous traffic. The trade-off is that it’s device/browser-specific, so the same user on two devices might be treated as two users.

If the change is not visible to users then the randomization unit will depend on what we want to measure. Eg - website performance changes, changes to backend algorithm

Another thing to consider is the coarseness of the randomization  unit and unit of analysis(what you are measuring like page views, clicks). If the ran. unit is too broad(eg-region/company) an you are measuring usrr level behavior the results will be misleading. Rule is to always randomize at the same level or finer level than unit of analysis.

If we randomize using a coarser unit, like location, but analyze at a finer level like user clicks, the results might be biased because group differences can come from the randomization unit itself, not the experiment.

How to estimate Sample Size?

sample size ~ 16*(sample variance)/(delta)**2; where delta is difference between control and treatment
We can use two-sample t-test to determine if the difference between two populations is statistically significant



Power - probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when the alternate is true. Likelihood of detecting an effect when the effect is present. The higher the power, the better the test is. It is commonly used to calculate the minimum sample size required to detect an effect.

P-peeking is the mistake of repeatedly checking p-values during an experiment and stopping early when significance is reached. This increases the chance of false positives(Type 1 error). To avoid it, we pre-define test duration or use statistically safe sequential testing methods.
